      <abstract>
        <para>
          &kexec; lets a running Linux kernel start another kernel directly, skipping firmware
          and boot loader initialization. &productname; uses this mechanism both for fast
          reboots and for &kdump;, which captures a memory snapshot (a vmcore file) whenever the
          kernel panics. Configuring crash kernel memory correctly, and knowing how to analyze a
          captured vmcore afterward, are essential skills for diagnosing kernel-level failures
          and reducing planned downtime.
        </para>
        <para><emphasis>Prerequisites:</emphasis></para>
        <itemizedlist>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              <literal>root</literal> or equivalent administrative privileges, to install
              packages, edit <filename>/etc/sysconfig/kdump</filename>, and reboot the system.
            </para>
          </listitem>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Enough free disk space at the dump target to hold a copy of system memory;
              &kdump; refuses to save a crash dump otherwise.
            </para>
          </listitem>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              On the immutable variant of &productnameshort;, familiarity with
              <command>transactional-update</command>, since &kdump; configuration there is
              applied through system snapshots.
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </itemizedlist>
      </abstract>
